'''Bucerotidæ''' (bū-se-rot′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Buceros'' (''-rot-'') + ''-idæ.''] The hornbills; a family of non-passerine insessorial birds of the warmer parts of the old world, having a huge bill, surmounted by a boss, casque, or horn, in some cases as large as the bill itself. {{smaller|The technical characters are a highly pneumatic skeleton, peculiar pterylosis, no aftershafts, hairy eyelashes, 10 rectrices, tufted elæodochon, no cæca, a gall-bladder, and sympelmous syndactylous feet. About 50 species are described, distributed in about 20 modern genera. See ''hornbill.'' Also called ''Buceridæ, Bucerontidæ.''}}

'''Buchanite''' (buk′an-īt), ''n.'' [< ''Buchan'' (see def.) + ''-ite''<sup>2</sup>.] One of a sect which arose in 1783, in the Relief Congregation at Irvine, Scotland, under the leadership of a Mrs. (more commonly known as Lucky) Buchan. {{smaller|She declared herself to be the woman of Rev. xii., and Mr. White, the pastor of the congregation to which she belonged, her “man-child”; and she taught her followers that they would be translated to heaven without tasting of death. The sect was always small, and is now extinct.}}

'''bucholzite''' (bö′kōlt-sit), ''n.'' [Named after the chemist ''Buchholz'' (1770-1818).] A variety of fibrolite.

'''buchu''' (bū′kū), ''n.'' [A native name; also spelled ''bucku.''] The leaves of several species of ''Barosma'', shrubby plants at the Cape of Good Hope, having an aromatic taste and penetrating odor, and extensively used in medicine for various disorders of the stomach and urinary organs. See ''Barosma.''

'''buck'''<sup>1</sup> (buk), ''n.'' [< ME. ''buk, bucke, bukke,'' a male deer; also, as orig., a he-goat; < AS. ''bucca,''

